# Line-ending and diff handling — narrow on purpose.
#
# There is deliberately NO `* text=auto` rule here. `legacy-wda-source-lock.json`
# records digests over source that has already been normalised CRLF -> LF (see
# AGENTS.md §3.18.1); a blanket normalisation rule would re-normalise the files
# those digests are taken over and skew every one of them. A rule that fixes one
# class of file by breaking another is not an improvement.
#
# So: name only what must not be touched, and leave everything else to git's own
# detection, which is what has been in effect until now.

# Shipped binaries whose bytes are pinned by SHA-256 in NOTICE and in
# sidecars/android/android-tools-manifest.json. git's NUL-byte heuristic already
# treats these as binary; saying it explicitly means a future `text=auto` cannot
# silently corrupt them.
*.apk binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.ipa binary

# The whole bundled-tool tree is byte-pinned, INCLUDING the text file in it.
#
# This line is load-bearing, and the reason is not obvious: this machine has
# `core.autocrlf = true`, and Google's NOTICE.txt for platform-tools is pure LF
# (21,893 bare LF, 0 CRLF). Without `-text`, checkout would rewrite every one of
# those to CRLF, the file would gain 21,893 bytes, and both the byte count and
# the SHA-256 pinned for it would be wrong on any fresh clone — while being
# perfectly right on the machine that wrote them. That is the failure mode where
# CI is green for the author and red for everybody else.
sidecars/android/** -text

# These reviewed knowledge packages record exact installed-byte provenance.
# Keep their hashes stable on Windows clones without renormalizing runtime assets.
.claude/skills/rust-best-practices/** text eol=lf
.claude/skills/systematic-debugging/** text eol=lf

